0               ______
+               ______
+               ______
+7. Overview of MAILER Operation
+7. Overview of MAILER Operation
+7. Overview of MAILER Operation
0                                          ______
+  This  is  a  brief  description of what MAILER does.  When first started,
0______
+MAILER reads the Mailer Profile, allocates various tables,  and  loads  any
0user  exits that are not built in.  It then goes into a wait state, waiting
0for the arrival  of  a  reader  file,  an  SMSG  interrupt,  or  a  console
0interrupt.
0
0  If  the  interrupt was an SMSG or from console typein, the command parser
0is invoked to process the user command.  For reader interrupts,  each  file
0in the reader is read and processed, usually resulting in the generation of
0outgoing punch spool files.
0
0  A valid reader file can be one of:
                                   - 10 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
    1. An RFC822 mail file.    A  mail  file  is  a  PUNCH  spool  file
       containing a header seperated by a blank record from the text of
       the message.  The syntax specification for the header format  is
                          ________ ___ ___ ______ __ ____ ________ ____
+      derived  from  the Standard for the Format of ARPA Internet Text
       ________
+      Messages, DARPA Request for Comments #822.  NOTE: Only a  subset
                                     ______
+      of  RFC822  is  supported  by MAILER.  Specifically, it fails to
       properly parse nested parentheses and route-lists.  The  various
                                               "RFCxxx  STANDARD"
+                                              "RFCxxx  STANDARD"
+      Internet   RFC's   are   available  as  "RFCxxx  STANDARD"  from
       NICSERVE@BITNIC.
0   2. A Batch Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (BSMTP) request.  A  BSMTP
       request is differentiated from an RFC822 mail file by having the
       first record start with the text "HELO ".   This  is  the  first
       command  of an SMTP dialog and looks sufficiently different from
       a mail file to be easily identified.
0   3. A reply to a BSMTP request previsouly generated.  A BSMTP  reply
       is  identified  by  having  the first record start with 3 digits
       (e.g. "220").
0
0                                          ______
+  For both mail files and BSMTP requests, MAILER does essentially the  same
0thing.  It parses the mail or BSMTP request to obtain a list of recipients.
0In the case of BSMTP, this is simple -- each recipient is identified  by  a
0RCPT  command.  For RFC822 mail, it is a little more difficult to determine
0_____
+which addresses should be acted upon.    This  is  one  reason  that  BSMTP
0                        ______
+support  was  added  to MAILER.  Another reson is that BSMTP has become the
0de-facto standard for interoperation of various mail systems on BITNET.
0
0  Various table entries in the Mailer Profile  determine  exactly  how  the
0mail  is  sent to the list of recipients.  Specifically, the INCOMING table
0                               ______
+lists which other mailers your MAILER knows about and will trust.    Anyone
0not  listed  in  the INCOMING table must send a mail file with a Sender: or
0From: field that is consistent with the file's RSCS origin node and userid.
0
0                  ______                                             ______
+  The function of MAILER is described  in  further  detail  in  the  MAILER
0                                  - 11 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
 _______ _____ ______
+Program Logic Manual, "PLM LISTING."
0
08. MAILER PROFILE
+8. MAILER PROFILE
+8. MAILER PROFILE
0       "MAILER PROFILE"
+       "MAILER PROFILE"
+  The  "MAILER PROFILE" CMS file contains the parameters used to initialize
0    ______
+the MAILER routing tables and set other variables and option  flags.    You
0may  want  to  add  or remove names from the list of special userids not to
0receive mail.  See section MG, below, for the procedures for modifying  and
0automatically regenerating the "MAILER PROFILE".
0
0                                                                    ______
+  You  can  specify  a different name for the Profile when invoking MAILER.
0The syntax is:
0
             __  __  __
+    MAILER [fn [ft [fm]]]
0
                                                  MAILER   PROFILE        *
+                         __   __        __       MAILER   PROFILE        *
+The default values  for  fn,  ft,  and  fm  are  MAILER,  PROFILE,  and  *,
0respectively.    If,  for  example, your system has shared dasd between two
0                                                     ______
+cpu's (as ours does), your "PROFILE EXEC" can invoke MAILER with  different
0Profile  names  depending  on  the cpuid.  This allows you to have a single
0                    ______
+disk containing the MAILER program, txtlib, and profiles.
0
0  The parts of the Mailer Profile are described below:
0
 8.1. Variable Declarations
+8.1. Variable Declarations
+8.1. Variable Declarations
0  You may declare a variable at any time within the Profile by entering:
0
     ________ ____ _____
+    variable_name=value
0
 For example:
0
                                   - 12 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
     rscsname=RSCS
0
0  All variables declared this way are then available to any  user  exit  to
0                                                                 ______
+obtain their value.  Some variable names have special meaning to MAILER:
0
 RSCSname        VMID  of  the  RSCS/Networking server.  Files received from
                 this userid are assumed to be from the RSCS network.    The
                 file's  TAG  text is parsed for the network origin node and
                 userid.  Files destined to the RSCS  network  are  sent  to
                 this VMID.
0
 RSCSalt         VMID of a secondary RSCS/Networking server.  This is useful
                 if you need to run two different RSCS's  for  some  reason.
                 ______
+                MAILER   will   always  send  files  to  the  primary  RSCS
                  ________
+                (rscsname), but will accept files from this RSCS  as  being
                 from the network.
0
                                                ______
+ThisNode        RSCS node name for the machine MAILER is running on.
0
 RSCS-Domain     The  default  domain name to concatenate with the RSCS node
                 name when domain-style names are needed.
0
                                                         ______
