 8.2. ORIGINS Table
+8.2. ORIGINS Table
+8.2. ORIGINS Table
0  Entries in the ORIGINS table are keyed by the VM userid.  Any reader file
0from  a  user  listed  in the table causes the associated ORIGINS user exit
0program to be invoked prior to any other processing of the file.  The  list
0of available ORIGINS exits is listed in section ORIGINS-EXITS below.
0
 8.3. INCOMING Table
+8.3. INCOMING Table
+8.3. INCOMING Table
0  Entries  in  the INCOMING table are keyed by RSCS origin node and userid.
0After a file is received and its mail header has been parsed, the  INCOMING
0user  exit  program is invoked.  A typical use of an INCOMING exit is to do
0validation of mail  to  prevent  forged  "From:"  userids.    The  list  of
0available INCOMING exits is listed in section INCOMING-EXITS below.
0
0                                  - 14 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
 8.4. OUTGOING Table
+8.4. OUTGOING Table
+8.4. OUTGOING Table
0  Entries  in  the  OUTGOING  table  are  keyed  by  host  name.   For this
0            ____ ____
+discussion, host name is defined as everything to the right of the  at-sign
0                                                  _____ _____
+                                                  _____ _____
+                                                  _____ _____
+                                                  EACUS@CUVMA  CUVMA
+                                                  EACUS@CUVMA  CUVMA
+in  a  mail address.  For example, in the address EACUS@CUVMA, CUVMA is the
0                                              ____ _____ _________ _______
+                                              ____ _____ _________ _______
+                                              ____ _____ _________ _______
+                                              John_Smith@Newcastle.MAILNET
+                                              John_Smith@Newcastle.MAILNET
+host   name   and,    in    the    address    John_Smith@Newcastle.MAILNET,
0Newcastle.MAILNET
+Newcastle.MAILNET
+Newcastle.MAILNET is the hostname.
0
0                                                        ______
+  When  mail  is  to  be  sent to a particular address, MAILER looks up the
0hostname in the OUTGOING table and then  invokes  the  OUTGOING  user  exit
0                                                        _____ _______ _____
+program.    If  the  hostname  can't  be  found and the Allow-Unknown-Nodes
0            True
+            True       ______
+variable is True, then MAILER looks for the special OUTGOING entry with the
0      *DEFAULT*
+      *DEFAULT*
+key  "*DEFAULT*"  and  invokes  the  user  exit  for  that entry.  (See the
0description of the DOMAINS table, below, for an exception).   The  list  of
0available OUTGOING exits is in section OUTGOING-EXITS, below.
0
 8.5. DOMAINS Table
+8.5. DOMAINS Table
+8.5. DOMAINS Table
0  The  DOMAINS  table  is  an  optional  table  that  has  the  same  basic
0functionality as the OUTGOING table.  The difference is that the key for an
0entry  in  the  DOMAINS table need not match the hostname exactly.  It need
0                                                                     ______
+only match the right-hand end.  When a DOMAINS  table  is  defined,  MAILER
0first  searces  the  OUTGOING  table  and,  failing  to find a match there,
0searches the DOMAINS table before finally giving  up  and  going  with  the
0"*DEFAULT*" entry.  The DOMAINS and OUTGOING tables use the same exits.
0
0
0
0
                                   - 15 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
 8.6. SPECIAL Table
+8.6. SPECIAL Table
+8.6. SPECIAL Table
0  The  key  to  an  entry  in  the  SPECIAL  table  is a username, that is,
0everything to the left of the at-sign in a mail address.  The SPECIAL table
0can  be used to implement userid aliases of up to 255 characters in length,
0mailing lists, automatic mail forwarding, etc.  Note that the SPECIAL table
0                             ______
+is  never  searched  by  the MAILER proper.  It is only used if an OUTGOING
0exit (like LOCAL) chooses to.  A list of SPECIAL user exits is  in  section
0SPECIAL-EXITS.
0
