 9.1. Outgoing Exits
+9.1. Outgoing Exits
+9.1. Outgoing Exits
0  These exit names are used in the Profile OUTGOING and DOMAINS tables.  In
0addition to these standard exits, several sites have implemented their  own
0local exits for various functions such as gatewaying mail over "home grown"
0or otherwise strange links to other systems.  Each exit has a  type  and  a
0(sometimes optional) 8 character parameter.
0
0  There  are  three types of OUTGOING exit which determine exactly when the
0exit is invoked and with what information.   Some  exits  may  be  used  as
0different  types  if  they are properly written although they generally are
0written to be of a specific type.  The three types are:
0
 1    Called once per recipient address.
0                                  - 16 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
 2    Called once for all recipients having the same server node/userid pair
      and exit name.
0
 3    Same  as  type  2  but  complete  information  about all recipients is
      available.  This includes having different exit  parameters  available
      to the same exit (e.g. BSMTP parameters).
0
0                                  ______
+  The OUTGOING exits provide with MAILER are:
0
 9.1.1. BITNET
+9.1.1. BITNET
+9.1.1. BITNET
0  This obsolete type 2 exit sends a file to another RSCS node assumed to be
0        ______
+running MAILER. The file is sent to the server node/userid  listed  in  the
0table.  BITNET has been superseded by the BSMTP exit.
0
 9.1.2. BSMTP
+9.1.2. BSMTP
+9.1.2. BSMTP
                                                               2
   This type 3 exit sends a Batch Simple Mail Transfer Protocol  data stream
0to  the server node/userid from the table.  A RCPT command is generated for
0each recipient that caused the exit to be invoked.  The usual  domain  name
0used   to  identify  the  sending  host  in  the  HELO  command  is  either
0_____ ______                         ________     ____ ______
+Local-Domain or the concatenation of ThisNode and RSCS-Domain.
0
0  The optional parameter to BSMTP can be one of:
0
 ___
+var             A variable name.  The value of the variable is used as  the
                 HELO domain instead of the default described above.
0
 @
+@___
+@var            A variable name preceded by an at-sign.  This is used to do
                 BSMTP routing to another BSMTP  server  that  supports  the
0
 _______________
0  2
    a  store-and-forward  version  of  the  ARPANET  SMTP service defined by
 J. Postel in DARPA Request for Comments #821
                                   - 17 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
                                            ______
+                BSMTP   source   route.    MAILER  does  not  support  this
                 construct, but others such as the Wiscnet SMTP  server  do.
                              ___
+                The value of var is inserted in the source route portion of
                 each RCPT TO: address.
0
 %
+%___
+%var            A variable name preceded by a percent-sign.  This  is  used
                 to  perform  the  ubiquitous "percent routing hack" that is
                 widely used but is not documented  in  any  mail  standard.
                 The RCPT TO address is rewritten by changing the at-sign to
                                                          ___
+                a percent and appending the The value of var to the end  of
                 the address.
0
 Note  that  for  the  at-sign  and percent forms of the parameter, that the
0variable name must be 7 characters or less.  For the other form,  the  name
0must be 8 characters or less.
0
0  Examples  of  the  four  flavors  of  BSMTP  (vanilla  and three types of
0parameter) follow:
0
     via20B=@CU20B.COLUMBIA.EDU
     viaDEC=@DECWRL.DEC.COM
     funnydom=BLAH.BLAH.EDU
      ...
     OUTGOING:
     ;domain  node   userid   exit   type parameter
0    CUVMA    CUVMA  MAILER   BSMTP  3
     CUTC20   CUVMA  SMTPSERV BSMTP  3    @via20B
     FROTZ    XYZZY  FUZZNET  BSMTP  3    funnydom
      ...
0    DOMAINS:
     .DEC     WISCVM SMTPSERV BSMTP  3    %viaDEC
      ...
0
0  Given the following sample addresses, these RCPT TO's would be created:
0
 JOE@CUVMA      RCPT TO:<JOE@CUVMA>
0JOE@CUTC20     RCPT TO:<@CU20B.COLUMBIA.EDU:JOE@CUTC20>
0                                  - 18 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
 JOE@DEC123.DEC RCPT TO:<JOE%DEC123.DEC@DECWRL.DEC.COM>
0
 9.1.3. DEFRT
+9.1.3. DEFRT
+9.1.3. DEFRT
0  This type 1 exit sends a file directly to the  intended  recipient  on  a
0                               ______
+remote  RSCS  node not running MAILER.  The file name is set to that of the
0sender (truncated to 8 characters).  The file type is set  to  "MAIL",  the
0class to "M", and the distribution code to the host name of the sender.
0
0  DEFRT  will  generally  refuse  to  deliver  mail  to userids more than 8
0characters long since this is the VM implementation  limit.    An  optional
0            TRUNCATE
+            TRUNCATE
+parameter,  TRUNCATE, may be supplied which allows longer names.  The first
08 characters are used in this case.  This facility is meant to be  used  in
0                                              ____
+conjunction  with  VAX/VMS  systems  running  jnet and UNIX systems running
0____
+UREP.  DEFRT ignores the server node/userid information in the table entry.
0
 9.1.4. LOCAL
+9.1.4. LOCAL
+9.1.4. LOCAL
0                                                                    ______
+  This type 1 exit sends mail to a local user on the system running MAILER.
0It sets the file name, etc. as in DEFRT above.  It also sets the file's TAG
0to look like one RSCS would generate.  This allows a  modified  RDRLIST  or
0RECEIVE exec to determine the origin of a remote file in the same manner as
0for files from RSCS.
0
0               PROFS
+               PROFS
+  The optional PROFS parameter will cause a  PROFS  Subject  record  to  be
0appended  to  the end of the file so that PROFS mail may be used.  Kudos to
0Peter Day of Emory University.  See also the PROFS SPECIAL exit.
0
0
0                                  - 19 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
