 9.3.5. PROFS
+9.3.5. PROFS
+9.3.5. PROFS
0  The PROFS exit does the same thing as the LOCAL OUTGOING  exit  with  the
0PROFS  parameter.  You would use this exit when most users get the standard
0LOCAL treatment but a specific list of users (which you  enumerate  in  the
0SPECIAL table) want mail delivered in PROFS format.
0
 9.3.6. (none)
+9.3.6. (none)
+9.3.6. (none)
0  Omitting  the  exit  name  causes  the  built in exit to be invoked which
0disallows mail to the given user name.  Use this approach to  prevent  mail
0from being sent to specific users like CMSBATCH and SYSTEM.
0
 9.4. Origins Exits
+9.4. Origins Exits
+9.4. Origins Exits
0  The  Origins table lists exits to be invoked based on origin userid.  The
0exit is given control immediately after the  file  has  been  received  and
0prior  to  any  other  processing  has  been performed.  This is unlike the
0Incoming exit which is not invoked until the mail header has been  read  in
0and  parsed.   An Origins exit can be used to do arbitrary processing of an
0input file based on origin userid.
0
 ____
+none            Leaving the exit name blank causes the built in exit to  be
                 invoked  which  causes the received file to be purged and a
                 message sent to the originating userid.  This is useful  to
                 dissallow  users  like  CMSBATCH  from sending any files to
                 ______
+                MAILER and guarantees that no error mail files will be sent
                 back to those users.
