 11.2.4. Cookbook
+11.2.4. Cookbook
+11.2.4. Cookbook
0                                      ______
+  The cookbook approach to build your MAILER Profile with MG follows.  This
0is done for you by the MCONFIG EXEC.
0
0                                  - 29 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
   The first time only:
0
                 ____
+   1. Create a "name MTPLATE" file similar to the one in figure 1.
0                ____
+   2. Create a "name MAILLCL" file with a single line in it:
0
           ________
+          SITENAME   *      *    LOCAL   1
0
    3. Get  yourself  added  onto  the list of people that are informed
       when a new copy of the various NAMES files arrive at  your  site
       (or you can "poll" NICSERVE@BITNIC for new versions).  The files
       are huge, so try to  find  them  on  your  system  first  before
       obtaining them from NICSERVE.
0
0  Each  time  a  new  "XMAILER",  "MAILER"  or  "DOMAIN NAMES" file becomes
0available:
0
    1. Obtain read access to the NAMES files on any accessed disk.
0                             ____               ____
+   2. Obtain read access to "name MTPLATE" and "name MAILLCL"  on  any
       accessed disk.
0   3. Type:
0
              ____
+          MG name
0
                                        ____
+   4. When  MG  is  done, a new file, "name PROFILE A1" will have been
       created.
0               ____
+   5. Compare "name PROFILE A1" to "MAILER PROFILE" on the  disk  that
       ______
+      MAILER  reads  when it starts up to check for any discrepancies.
       You should see only a list of new BITNET sites plus  some  sites
                     ______
+      that have put MAILER into production use recently.
0                                                ____
+   6. If  you  are  satisfied  with  the  new  "name PROFILE", replace
       "MAILER  PROFILE"  with  it  (after  making  a   backup   copy).
       Occasionally,  the  data  provided  by  BITNIC  is incorrect and
       ______
+      MAILER will find a fatal error in the new PROFILE  so  you  will
       have to revert to the previous version.
0   7. Tell  Mailer  to  re-access  the  disk  reload  (see the example
       below).
0                                  - 30 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
                                                                  ______
+  The suggested way to install a  new  "MAILER  PROFILE"  while  MAILER  is
0running is as follows:
0
    1. Obtain  write  access  to  the  minidisk  containing the "MAILER
                              ______
+      PROFILE" (assuming the MAILER has read-only access).
0   2. Make the needed changes to the file.
0           ______
+   3. Tell MAILER to reaccess the disk.  For example, if the  disk  is
       linked  at 193 as the B disk, issue the following commands (User
       typein prefixed by "."):
0
           .CP SMSG MAILER CMS ACC 193 B
           | CMS command accepted.
           | R(00000) for: ACC 193 B
           .CP SMSG MAILER RELOAD
           | Reload command accepted.
0
       (These commands will only be accepted by the  userid  listed  as
           ______
+      the MAILER maintainer in the profile.)
0
012. Diagnosing Problems
+12. Diagnosing Problems
+12. Diagnosing Problems
0  ______
+  MAILER  breaks  from time to time.  Typically, some error will occur from
0which it thinks it can't recover, so it will exit.  This generally leads to
0______
+MAILER  logging out if you use the standard PROFILE EXEC.  If this happens,
0                                   ______ _
+take a look at the topmost file in Mailer's reader.  This  is  usually  the
0culprit.    Most  errors  have to do with wild syntax in a file that causes
0______
+MAILER to crash due to some  "impossible  case"  happening  in  the  header
0parsing module (which is admittedly a sloppy job).
0
0                               ______
+  Transfer this file away from MAILER and log it back in.  It will probably
0run just fine.  Take a look at the file and if you can't figure out why  it
0        ______
+caused  MAILER  to break down, then try asking someone else on BITNET (such
0as the members of the XMAILER or MAIL-L mailing lists).
                                   - 31 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
 13. Messages
+13. Messages
+13. Messages
0  ______
+  MAILER generates messages on the  console,  as  messages  sent  to  users
0within  mail  reporting  an error, and as BSMTP replies.  Most messages use
0                           __________       ___              ______  ___
+the standard CMS format of CCCMMMNNNT where CCC is "MAI" for MAILER, MMM is
0                 ______                                  ___
+the  particular  MAILER  module  issuing  the  message,  NNN is the message
0            _
+number, and T is one of three standard message  types  and  two  additional
0types:
0
 I               Informational.
0
 W               Warning.
0
 E               Error.
0
 U               Report error to user within error mail.
0
 X               Send  RSCS  MSG  to  mail  sender  (e.g.  who  the  mail is
                 "From:").
0
0  The message code is never put in error mail or sent as a CP or  RSCS  MSG
0but is written to the console depending on the CP EMSG setting.
0
 13.1. Message Codes
+13.1. Message Codes
+13.1. Message Codes
0                                                                     ______
+  The  following  messages  with  message  numbers  are generated by MAILER
0proper and the various user exits:
0
                                   __ __ __      ____
+MAILER000E Unable to obtain file 'fn ft fm', RC=nnnn
0
                                    _______  ____
+MAILER001I CUCCA VM Network MAILER version  date  Copyright  (c)  1982,1987
     Columbia University
0
 MAILER002E Routing file not lrecl 80 and recfm F.
                                   - 32 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
                              _______
+MAILER003E Stack overflow in routine
     ______
+    MAILER did not have adequate virtual storage available to  process  the
     mail.   This may be caused by a garbage file in the reader, a mail file
     with a very large header, or the virtual machine size is too small.
0
                              ____
+MAILER004I MAILER running on node
0
 MAILER005I Initialization complete.
0
 MAILER006I MAILER waiting for work.
0
                                         __
+MAILER007E MAILER stopping due to error nn
     Look  for  a previous error message which indicates the specific nature
     of the error.
0
                       ____
+MAILER008E DIAG 14 RC=nnnn
     CP  Diagnose  X'14'  failed.    This "never happens" except when the CP
     SPOOL is in a mess.
0
                          _______         ____       ____
+MAILER009I Received file spoolid sender: vmid from: node <userid>
0
                      ____
+MAILER010E RDCARD RC=nnnn
                                     ___ _______ ___ _____ _________
+    See  message 008, above and the CMS Command and Macro Reference for the
     RDCARD macro return codes.
0
            ____
+MAILER011I nnnn bytes of stack space.
                                                                      ______
+    After  initializing  (allocating tables and loading user exits), MAILER
     allocates the rest of available contiguous virtual storage for use as a
     stack for mail processing tasks.
0
             ____
+MAILER012I 'name' not in routing table.
0
                          ____     ____
+MAILER013W Distrib exit 'name' RC=nnnn
     The named exit set a non-zero return code.  See if the  exit  generated
     any error messages preceding this one.
0
                              ____ ____        ______
+MAILER014W Invalid entry in 'file name', line lineno
0
                            __       ______
+MAILER015W Table add error nn, line lineno
     An error code was returned from the function that adds entries  to  one
                                   - 33 -
+
1                    ______
+                    MAILER Installation and Maintenance
0
                             ______                      ______
+    of  the  several tables MAILER uses.  See what line lineno contains and
     hopefully that will tell you what went wrong.
0
            ____
+MAILER016E name table is empty
0
                       ____             __
+MAILER017E User exit 'name' load error nn
     __                                                   ______
+    Nn  is  the  return code from the CMS LOAD command.  MAILER dynamically
                                         ____         ____ _______
+    loads user exits by issuing a "LOAD name (ORIGIN some address).   These
     exits  must  not  contain any unresolved external references (much like
     RSCS exits).  (Exit developers should see the CMS PRELOAD command).
0
                       ____            ___ _______
+MAILER018I User exit 'name' loaded at hex address
     Useful for determining the source of abends.
0
                                    ____ ______
+MAILER019U Unknown network sender: node userid
     A user not registered as a trusted mailer tried to send mail containing
     other than his own node name and userid as the sender.  See also mesage
     021.
0
 MAILER020U Outgoing mail has no FROM: or SENDER: field.
     The RFC822 mail header is missing a required field.
0
 MAILER021U FROM: or SENDER: inconsistent with spool file origin.
     See message 019.
0
                               ____       ____ ____
+MAILER022E Invalid exit type 'type' for 'exit name'
0
 MAILER023U Required DATE is not present.
